window.docContents[231].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Elliptic-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Fundamental-solutions-and-classical-harmonic-functions--Fundamental-solutions":{"contents":"The canonical Laplace kernel is locally integrable and is smooth and harmonic\naway from its pole. For , its radial derivative has the normalized\nunit flux across spheres centered at the pole. The off-pole statements hold\nin every positive dimension; the distributional equation needs more than the\nflux normalization and is proved by a separate argument in each of three cases.\n\n* In dimension one, the scalar kernel  and its canonical\n encoding are proved equal. The associated regular\ndistribution satisfies .* In dimension two, the logarithmic kernel is regularized, its finite-order\ntest-function identity is proved, and the ordinary distribution satisfies\n.* For , the Newtonian kernel satisfies\n for compactly supported\n test functions. Spherical means, the ball divergence theorem, and\ncoarea give the pole identity, including the limit at the origin.\nIts regular distribution therefore satisfies .\n\nFor every , convolution with a compactly supported  datum\nproduces a classical  solution of .\nDifferentiation under the integral transfers the Laplacian to the datum,\nand the corresponding planar or Newtonian pole identity gives the equation.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Elliptic Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Fundamental solutions and classical harmonic functions","header":"5.7.1.1.1. Fundamental solutions","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Elliptic-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Fundamental-solutions-and-classical-harmonic-functions--Fundamental-solutions"},"/Elliptic-Equations/Model-Dirichlet-Problems-and-Harmonic-Rigidity/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Model-Dirichlet-Problems-and-Harmonic-Rigidity--Maximum___-Minimum___-Comparison___-and-Uniqueness-for-Harmonic-Functions--Harnack-comparison":{"contents":"The maximum principle compares an interior value with boundary data. Harnack\ncompares two interior values using positivity. If ,\nthen , and mean values and  give\n\n\n\nPositivity is the step that permits enlarging the integration region.\nA function that changes sign can lose mass by cancellation, so the same\nargument would fail. This also explains why a nonnegative harmonic function\ncannot vanish at an interior point and be positive just beside it.\n\nOn the unit disc the Poisson kernel makes the comparison exact. Every\npositive harmonic function is an average of the kernels \nover boundary points , and each kernel satisfies\n\n\n\n\n\nThe figure plots these ratios along one radius. The band is the whole of the\nHarnack inequality on the disc. On  it gives\n, and the constant blows up like  as the\nsmaller disc grows toward the boundary. The formal proof chains mean-value\nballs instead of using the Poisson kernel, which gives a worse constant on any\ncompact set but works in every domain.\n\nLet , , and suppose  is nonnegative and harmonic on a set\ncontaining . If , thenThe ambient harmonicity set need not be open: the explicit closed-ball\ncontainment supplies every mean-value ball used in the proof.\n\nThe ball  lies inside . Nonnegativity makes the integral\nover the smaller ball no larger than the integral over the larger one.\nApplying the ball mean-value formula at  and , then using\n, gives the stated factor.\n\nTo compare distant points, we pass through a chain of overlapping balls, each\nlink costing a factor . The constant can deteriorate as the compact set\napproaches the boundary or as the connecting geometry forces more links.\n\nLet , let  be open and preconnected, and let\n be compact. There is a constant , depending only on\n, , and , such that every nonnegative harmonic function on\n satisfies\n\nJoin points of the connected domain by chains of overlapping admissible\nballs. Each link contributes the local factor . Compactness gives a\nfinite subcover of  and hence a uniform bound on the number of links\nneeded from a fixed base point. Comparing both  and  with that base\npoint produces one constant valid for every pair in .\n\nThe same inequality holds for nonnegative solutions of  with variable\ncoefficients, which is Theorem 5 of  §6.4.3.\nThere is no mean-value formula in this setting. The Lean proof uses the\ncontact-set method, with the monotone envelope, contact Jacobian, and basic\nmeasure estimate following Imbert and Silvestre's lecture notes on fully\nnonlinear equations (§§4.1 to 4.2 of the 2013 HAL version). No derivative of\na coefficient is taken anywhere.\n\nThe first step is a quantitative form of the strong principle: a nonnegative\nsupersolution without potential that is small at one point is small on a set\nof definite measure.\n\nSuppose that on  the quadratic form of  is at least\n with , , and\n. Let  be continuous on ,\n in , and satisfy  there with . If\n for some , then\n\nSlide each paraboloid with vertex  up until\nit touches  from below on . Because\n and  on the unit ball, the touching happens at an\ninterior point where . The contact set is compact and the map\nsending a contact point to its vertex covers the vertex ball. At a contact point\nthe contact Jacobian  is positive semidefinite, and  together with\nthe gradient identity at contact gives\n. This bounds\n by , and the area formula gives the estimate.\n\nCovering the unit cube by dyadic subcubes and applying this estimate at every\nscale turns one point of smallness into geometric decay of the superlevel sets.\nThis is the  estimate, and it controls  from below.\n\nLet , , and . There are  and\n such that the following holds for all coefficients with\nellipticity , , and\n on the ball of radius . If  is\n there, satisfies  with , and  at some\npoint of the closed unit cube , then for every \n\nThe contact estimate, rescaled to cubes, says that  on a fixed\nfraction  of a cube whenever  at a point of a slightly larger\ncube. Apply it to . A dyadic child in which  has\ndensity above  cannot contain a point of its parent where\n. Mathlib's subbox partition theorem turns this into the induction\nthat loses the factor  at each level.\n\nA matching upper estimate for subsolutions and a chain of cubes across the\ndomain finish the proof. The signed potential is absorbed by multiplying  by an\nexponential in an added coordinate, which turns  into a drift in one more\ndimension.\n\nLet  be open and bounded and let  be preconnected with\n. Let , ,  be continuous on  with\nbounded ranges, with  symmetric and\n for almost every , where\n. There is  such that every  with \nand  in  satisfiesThe potential  may have either sign. The second Lean statement replaces\nthe bounded ranges by essential bounds.\n\nContinuity turns the almost-everywhere bounds and ellipticity into pointwise\nones on the open set . Compactness of  and connectedness of\n give a finite chain of small balls in . On each ball the level decay\nbounds the infimum from below and the upper estimate bounds the supremum, with\nconstants fixed by , the coefficient bounds, and the radius. Multiplying\nalong the chain gives one constant for all pairs of points of .\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Model Dirichlet Problems and Harmonic Rigidity\u0009Maximum, Minimum, Comparison, and Uniqueness for Harmonic Functions","header":"5.3.4.3. Harnack comparison","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/Model-Dirichlet-Problems-and-Harmonic-Rigidity/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Model-Dirichlet-Problems-and-Harmonic-Rigidity--Maximum___-Minimum___-Comparison___-and-Uniqueness-for-Harmonic-Functions--Harnack-comparison"},"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Transport-and-Classical-Waves/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Transport-and-Classical-Waves--Classical-Wave-Equations":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Transport and Classical Waves","header":"7.2.3. Classical Wave Equations","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Transport-and-Classical-Waves/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Transport-and-Classical-Waves--Classical-Wave-Equations"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Certified-Bounds-and-Models/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Certified-Bounds-and-Models--Derivative-Bounds-and-Adaptive-Search":{"contents":"High-order quadrature and Taylor remainders require uniform bounds on the\nanalytic derivative. Checked symbolic derivatives are identified with\nMathlib's iteratedDerivWithin and then enclosed panel by panel on a declared\nreal domain.\n\nAdaptive search consumes only these proved local bounds. It may accept a\npanel, subdivide it, compare two certified trees, or exhaust its depth budget.\nThe search strategy is computational; the returned tree is useful because its\nroot error theorem is independent of that strategy.\n\nFrom  to an analytic bound.\n\nLet the symbolic expression be . Repeated checked differentiation\nand normalization produce the constant fourth derivative 24. The\nvariable-independent route can evaluate that expression once, obtaining the\ndegenerate enclosure , and reuse it on every requested panel.\n\nThe route then crosses two proof boundaries:\n\n1. the differentiation theorem identifies the checked symbolic expression\nwith Mathlib's iteratedDerivWithin 4;2. the interval theorem turns membership in  into an absolute-value\nupper bound.\n\nThe resulting IteratedDerivativeBounderOn proves, for every nondegenerate\npanel  in its declared domain and every ,\n\n\n\nFor the polynomial, smoothness is proved analytically, and a quadrature\ntheorem receives that ContDiffOn proof separately from the numerical bound.\n\nA rational example with a side condition.\n\nFor  on , the fourth derivative simplifies to\n. The checked rational route also records the condition\n. Interval positivity proves that condition uniformly because\n on the domain.\n\nA range provider can then enclose the simplified derivative. Even a\nconservative enclosure such as  is sufficient: converting it to a\nbound certificate yields the useful conclusion\n\n\n\nAn IteratedDerivativeBounderOn n f s produces a BoundCert for\n on every requested nondegenerate compact interval\ncontained in s. The specialized interface stores only the scalar panel bound\nand its direct proof for Mathlib's iteratedDerivWithin; its generic\nBoundCert is a derived projection. Constructors accept a panelwise Lean\nestimate, CEGA derivative enclosures, or any sound RealIntervalBackend.\nSmoothness is a separate analytic hypothesis; an enclosure of a formal\nderivative says nothing about differentiability of the original function.\n\nOn a set with UniqueDiffOn ℝ s, assume validity of the intermediate symbolic\nderivatives at levels . The order- checked derivative then denotes\nMathlib's iteratedDerivWithin. This includes every nondegenerate closed\ninterval contained in the validity domain. The linked regularity theorem\nuses the sufficient stronger assumption of validity through level \ninclusive to prove ContDiffOn ℝ n: it establishes continuity of the final\nderivative by proving that derivative differentiable.If  is the expression obtained by  checked symbolic\nderivatives, then on every admissible panel  and every ,\n\nInduct on the derivative order. The base case is evaluation itself. At the\nsuccessor step, apply the one-step symbolic derivative bridge to the current\nderived expression and use the induction hypothesis to identify the analytic\nwithin-derivative already constructed. The validity assumptions propagate to\nevery intermediate expression.\n\nThe rational route proposes a polynomial-gcd cancellation, checks its semantic\nequality through the exact normalizer, and turns generated denominator\nconditions into interval obligations. Exact rational hulls cover arbitrary\nreal panels, and checked zero exclusion or an absolute lower bound discharges\nthe nonzero conditions on the whole declared domain.Writing the checked route as , its semantic\nboundary is the conditional identityThe interval phase proves the premise uniformly on the panel.\n\nPolynomial gcd cancellation proposes the reduced rational expression, but the\nexact normalizer proves the cross-multiplied identity. Checked interval facts\nthen show that every cancelled denominator is nonzero throughout the panel.\nSubstituting those side facts into the conditional normalization theorem gives\nthe pointwise equality used by the derivative bounder.\n\nA verified panel evaluator, checked variable boxes, and a proof of successful\nevaluation define a total range provider for one symbolic expression. The\nprovider stores the returned backend interval together with its direct\nenclosure theorem.For every  with , its total contract is\n\nThe direct constructor encloses the structurally normalized symbolic\nderivative. A second constructor accepts an explicitly proved rewrite before\nrange evaluation. In both cases the resulting certificate bounds Mathlib's\nanalytic iterated derivative.For every accepted panel, the produced bound  satisfies\n\nSide-aware constructors consume a checked equality route together with proofs\nof every emitted nonzero condition. The rational specialization may cancel\npolynomial factors, but its simplified derivative enters the bounder only\nthrough that conditional semantic equality.The constructor composes\n\nWhen the compiled derivative contains no occurrence of the interval variable,\none verified evaluation at an enclosure of zero supplies the same bound on\nevery real panel. This avoids asking a finite interval backend to represent\narbitrary real endpoints for a constant expression.Variable independence givesfor every ; a single enclosure of the right-hand side is therefore valid\non every requested panel.\n\nA successful adaptive result contains a quadrature tree together with a proof\nthat the sum of its leaf errors is at most the root tolerance. Projecting this\nresult gives the ordinary interval-integral estimate for the tree's accumulated\nvalue.For , the public theorem is\n\nThe quadrature tree's soundness theorem bounds the root error by the sum of its\nleaf budgets. The dependent result type stores a proof that this accumulated\nbudget is at most ; transitivity gives the displayed estimate.\n\nThe generic depth-bounded search takes a proved local panel builder and\ninterval integrability on every nondegenerate panel in the domain. It accepts\na panel when its certificate meets the local tolerance, even at depth zero.\nOtherwise it bisects and gives each child half the budget if depth remains;\nwith no depth left, it returns none. This generic definition compares real\nerror bounds; the rational-budget interface supplies exact rational stopping\ncomparisons.Every successful search satisfies\n\nAdaptive Simpson search instantiates the generic engine with sharp local\nSimpson certificates built from panelwise fourth-derivative bounds.Every successful result therefore proveswith each accepted leaf justified by the fourth-order Simpson bound.\n\nAdaptive two-point Gauss–Legendre search uses the same subdivision policy with\nthe corresponding sharp Peano-kernel certificate at each accepted leaf.Its successful-result contract is likewise\n\nWhy subdivision can improve a certificate.\n\nFor  on , the fourth derivative is . The root panel\nmay therefore receive the bound , while its left half receives the\nsharper bound .\nIf the root certificate exceeds its assigned tolerance, adaptive search\nbisects the interval and asks the same derivative-bound object for each child.\n\nThe sharp Simpson budget on a panel of width  is\n, where  bounds the fourth derivative. Thus the unsplit\nbudget is , while the two half-panel budgets are\n\n\n\nTheir sum is . Most of the gain comes from the fifth power of the\npanel width; the smaller derivative bound on the left improves it further.\n\nIn this construction, both children meet tolerance , and\nadding the child certificates gives an estimate with analytic error\n. The arithmetic that chooses to split plays no part in the proof,\nwhich uses only the two local error bounds, interval\nintegrability, and additivity of the interval integral.\n\nA rational-budget candidate contains a client result, an exact rational upper\nbound for its semantic error, and a proof relating the two.\nWhen decidable rational comparison accepts the candidate, its real error is at\nmost the requested rational tolerance.Precisely,\n\nThe candidate's proof bounds its semantic real error by the cast of its exact\nrational budget. Boolean acceptance is precisely the decidable rational\ncomparison of that budget with . Casting this comparison to the reals\nand composing the inequalities proves the result.\n\nThe generic rational adaptive kernel keeps endpoints, midpoints, and local\ntolerances exact. A rejected or unavailable parent candidate is bisected at\nits rational midpoint; accepted children are combined only through the\nclient's proved error-subadditivity theorem.The dependent result type records the invariant\n\nThe quadrature specialization stores accepted local QuadratureCerts in the\nsame backend-independent tree used by real-valued adaptive search. Local\ninterval-integrability evidence justifies composing adjacent panel integrals.\nBoth an over-budget root and a missing root candidate may therefore be\nrecovered by proved child certificates, while a failed finite search returns\nnone.On success it returns exactly the root theoremwhere , , and  are the input rationals cast to reals.\n\nCompeting subdivision trees for the same interval can be compared by their\nproved error budgets. The selector returns a tree with the minimum budget.\nMore generally, a dependent leaf-refinement rule may propose a replacement\ntree at every accepted leaf; retaining the better local alternative is proved\nnever to increase the accumulated root error. Candidate generation is\nuntrusted.For two root trees ,and leafwise improvement satisfies .\n\nFor two complete trees, choose the one whose stored error budget is smaller;\nits existing soundness theorem supplies the integral estimate. For leafwise\nrefinement, use structural induction on the original tree. At each leaf retain\nthe original certificate or a proved-better replacement, and at each branch\nadd the two inductive error inequalities.\n\nA candidate family associates mesh, rule, or arithmetic-schedule keys with\nordinary quadrature certificates and an executable Boolean acceptance test.\nThe policy proves that every accepted result meets the requested tolerance.\nFinite search returns the first accepted key together with that theorem, or\nnone if every candidate fails.If finite search returns an accepted key , its projection proves\n\nFinite search returns a key only together with evidence that the family's\nacceptance predicate holds for that key. The policy theorem converts this\nevidence into the requested tolerance bound, and the candidate's stored\nquadrature soundness identifies the quantity being bounded. A failed search\nhas no conclusion to prove.\n\nExact-rational candidate families obtain their Boolean test from rational\ncomparison and transport the result to a real error theorem. A FloatLib\nenclosure of the exact rule value gives another such candidate: its certified\nendpoint radius bounds replacement by the midpoint, and that radius is added\nto the analytic error. For rounded endpoints, the radius must use their exact\ndecoded values. The derivative enclosure must still bound the analytic\nderivative on the whole panel: order four supplies the Simpson remainder,\nand order  supplies the degree- Taylor remainder.\n\nThe common derivative object is used by the Simpson and two-point\nGauss–Legendre constructors for one panel, fixed composites, planned\ntolerances, and adaptive subdivision. The total tolerance planner reserves\npart of the budget for analytic error and accepts a replacement value only\nwhen its proved numerical error fits the remainder.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Certified Bounds and Models","header":"10.7.4. Derivative Bounds and Adaptive Search","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Certified-Bounds-and-Models/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Certified-Bounds-and-Models--Derivative-Bounds-and-Adaptive-Search"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Exact-Symbolic-Calculus/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Exact-Symbolic-Calculus--Verified-Exact-Symbolic-Computation":{"contents":"An exact residual proof often ends with a cancellation. To establish\n on a domain , we can normalize\n to zero and prove that  throughout\n. Polynomial arithmetic can settle the first question; a sign,\ninterval, or root-exclusion argument can settle the second. The domain\ndetermines where we may use the resulting equality.\n\nThe normalization and polynomial-root certificates of the exact core cover\nconditional cancellation, real powers and exponentials, exact polynomial\ndivision, and Sturm chains. The calculus bridges use such identities to\njustify derivatives and antiderivatives.\n\n\n\nThe core expression type is an inductive syntax tree indexed by its variable\ntype. It contains rational constants, arithmetic operations, natural powers,\nreal powers with an arbitrary real exponent (Mathlib's Real.rpow),\ntrigonometric functions, exponential and logarithmic functions, square roots,\nand the inverse tangent, sine, and cosine. Evaluation uses Lean's total\nreal functions. A separate validity predicate records sufficient conditions\nfor their analytic rules: a nonzero denominator or logarithm argument, a\npositive square-root argument or power base, and an inverse sine or cosine\nargument different from . These conditions are needed by the\ncalculus bridges; an equality about total values need not require them.Schematically, the reflected language and its semantics areHere , the exponent  of  is a natural\nnumber while  in  is an arbitrary expression, and rational\nliterals with zero denominator are rejected by the well-formedness layer.\n\nIf the executable normalizer reduces an expression to literal zero, evaluating\nthe original expression in any real environment gives zero. The computation\ntherefore proposes a normal form, while this theorem is the kernel-checked\nsemantic justification.\n\nThe normalizer is accompanied by an invariant equating evaluation of each\nintermediate form with evaluation of the input. Specializing that invariant\nto a returned literal zero identifies the original value with the evaluation\nof zero, hence with zero in every environment.\n\nRational cancellation and other conditional rewrites return the nonzero\nconditions they require. Under those conditions, reduction to zero again\nimplies equality of the original real expression.If normalization returns a zero expression together with side conditions\n, then\n\nThe side-aware route proves evaluation preservation conditionally on every\nemitted nonzero or branch requirement. Once SideHolds supplies those\nrequirements, the route's final zero expression evaluates to zero, so the\noriginal expression does as well.\n\nEvery checked symbolic transformation is recorded by one route type. A\nSideClaimRoute stores the source and target expressions, the list of\nexpressions that must not vanish, and a proof that the target and source\nevaluate equally wherever that list holds; a positivity requirement is encoded\nas the nonvanishing of a square root. An unconditional transformation is the\nsame object with an empty list of assumptions.For a route with source , target , and assumption list ,\n\nThe side-conditioned pipeline contains a pass for the algebra of real powers\nand of the exponential, which it treats as the power of . Inside\na product or quotient all powers of one base collect into a single power,\nnested powers flatten, powers of products distribute, exponents are put into\na polynomial normal form, and a literal exponent is read back as a natural\npower, a reciprocal, or a square root. Each rewrite that Mathlib states for a\npositive base records the positivity of that base as a condition unless the\nbase is structurally positive (a positive numeral, , , an\nexponential, or a product of these); rewrites valid for the total\nReal.rpow, and the exponential identities, record nothing.Writing  for the output of the pass on ,\n\nEach local rewrite uses the corresponding identity for real powers, square\nroots, or exponentials under its recorded positivity conditions. The\nbottom-up traversal retains every condition from the rewrites it applies. Induction over\nthe traversal composes the local evaluation equalities into the equality for\nthe whole tree; when the expression contains no power atom the pass returns it\nunchanged.\n\nExact interval evaluation encloses the value of a sparse rational polynomial\nat every real point of a rational panel. The root checks first try the\ncentred form, the exact Taylor shift of the polynomial to the midpoint of the\npanel bounded term by term over its radius, and use termwise evaluation when\nthe centred interval does not exclude zero. This theorem is the semantic basis\nfor both root exclusion and derivative-sign checks.\n\nTermwise evaluation encloses each monomial with interval multiplication and\nnatural powers and combines the terms with interval addition. The basic\ninterval operations preserve membership, so induction over the sparse term\nlist shows that the real polynomial value lies between the computed rational\nendpoints. For the centred form, write  with the\nexact shifted coefficients , where  is the midpoint and  the\nradius of the panel. Since , an even power  lies in\n and an odd one in , so every term lies in a rational\ninterval and their sum encloses . The enclosure returns one of these\ntwo sound intervals.\n\nIf checked interval evaluation excludes zero, the polynomial is nonzero\nthroughout the panel. The result can be used directly as a side condition for\nan ordinary real-expression theorem.\n\nSound interval evaluation places every  in the computed range.\nThe Boolean root-free test proves that zero is outside that range. Therefore\nno enclosed value can equal zero.\n\nFor example, on , interval arithmetic gives\n. This one enclosure proves that the denominator\n never vanishes there and also gives\n. The nonvanishing fact justifies an exact\ncancellation; the two numerical endpoints control the size of the\nreciprocal. Exact reasoning and CEGA draw on the same domain information.\n\nEndpoint values satisfying , or the reversed\ninequalities, give a root by the intermediate value theorem. A checked\nstrict derivative sign makes the polynomial strictly monotone in the\ncorresponding direction. Together they prove that the rational bracket\ncontains exactly one real root, possibly at an endpoint.For every valid bracket certificate,\n\nContinuity and the weak endpoint inequalities give existence by the intermediate\nvalue theorem. The derivative enclosure has one strict sign on the whole\npanel, so the mean value theorem makes  strictly monotone there. A\nstrictly monotone function has at most one zero, proving uniqueness.\n\nA root atlas combines root-free and uniquely rooted panels with a proved\ncover. Every real root in the outer domain therefore belongs to one of the\natlas's isolated root regions.If  is an atlas over , its projection isFor a global atlas the premise  is removed by its outer-root bound.\n\nTake a root in the covered domain and choose an atlas panel containing it.\nA root-free panel is impossible because its certificate excludes zero.\nConsequently the chosen panel is one of the root regions, which proves the\ncovering conclusion. The global version first uses the outer bound to place\nthe root in the covered domain.\n\nA checked rational Cauchy certificate gives an explicit outer interval\ncontaining every real root. Its exact monic decomposition is verified before\nthe resulting coefficient bound is used.For its derived rational bound ,\n\nUse the checked monic decomposition to rewrite a hypothetical root equation.\nIf  were at least the computed coefficient bound, the leading term would\nstrictly dominate the sum of all lower-degree terms, contradicting that their\nsum is zero. Hence every root lies inside .\n\nUnderstanding a root certificate.\n\nThe isolated root need not be rational. A root atlas identifies it by a\nrational interval and a uniqueness theorem, which is often exactly what a PDE\nside-condition proof needs. The covering and uniqueness theorems of the\nRootAtlas and GlobalRootAtlas interfaces can discharge nonvanishing,\ndomain, and monotonicity side conditions in later symbolic or PDE arguments.\nThose theorems have the same form whether an atlas was assembled from\ninterval exclusion, Sturm subdivision, or both.\n\nThe Cauchy root bound in detail.\n\nFor a polynomial with rational coefficients, write it in monic form\n\n\n\nwhere  is the leading scalar. The checked bound is\n\n\n\nA CauchyBoundCertificate stores only the decomposition fields (scale ,\ndegree , and the lower-degree tail). The bound  is computed from that\ntail. An independent checker\nvalidates:\n\n1. the scale is nonzero;2. every exponent in the tail is strictly below ;3. the sparse identity  holds after normalization.\n\nWhen , the checked tail is empty and , so there are no\nroots. For , the soundness proof (abs_lt_bound) proceeds by\ncontradiction. If  and , dividing by  gives\n. Since , each lower-degree term\nsatisfies . Hence\n\n\n\nDividing by the positive factor  gives\n, a contradiction. The Lean proof implements this\nleading-term estimate with exact sparse evaluation and cancellation by the\nchecked nonzero scale.\n\nExample: isolating roots of .\n\nConsider the polynomial . A hand calculation shows the\ncertificate data; the generator may choose different split points.\n\n1. Cauchy bound. In monic form the tail is , whose coefficient\nabsolute values sum to . The checked bound is ,\nso all roots lie in .2. Square-free reduction. The gcd of  and  is , so\np is already square-free and its radical is p itself.3. Sturm chain construction.* ,* ,* Euclidean division gives\n, so\n, a nonzero constant.The terminal polynomial  has zero derivative and nonzero value,\nhence is globally nonzero (proved using the mean value theorem).4. Variation counting. At , the signs are , giving\ntwo sign changes. At , they are , giving none. The\ndrop  confirms that p has exactly two distinct real\nroots.5. Bisection. At , the signs are , giving one sign\nchange. Thus  and . The intervals\n and  each contain exactly one root.6. Atlas assembly. The global root atlas records the two isolated intervals,\ntogether with the checked Sturm certificate that proves their root counts.\n\nThe Sturm chain algorithm.\n\nA Sturm chain for a polynomial  is a sequence  satisfying:\n\n\n\nwhere the last element  is a nonzero constant. This signed Euclidean\nremainder sequence is the ordinary Euclidean algorithm with the sign of each\nremainder reversed. The certificate checker (SturmCertificate.valid)\nvalidates every recurrence by exact polynomial arithmetic, independently of\nthe generator that proposed the chain. The variation count  is the\nnumber of sign changes in , zeros omitted;\nSturm's theorem turns its drop\nbetween two endpoints into a root count.\n\nThe subdivision certificate.\n\nA Sturm subdivision is a binary tree of rational intervals over a search\ninterval, such as the Cauchy-bounded : every leaf has Sturm count at\nmost one, and every split is at a rational point that is not a root. SymLean\nchecks such a tree when a caller supplies it and has no generator for it; the\nroot and sign commands use their own root-cover search and root-list checkers.\nLeaves whose Sturm count is one are the isolated root regions.\n\nSparse polynomial representation.\n\nThe polynomial subsystem uses Verified.Expr.Alg.MvPoly n, a raw list of\nrational coefficient–monomial pairs. A monomial is a function\n recording the exponent of each variable.\nThe type permits repeated monomials and zero coefficients. The executable\nnormalization combines terms, removes zeros, and orders the result without\nchanging the polynomial's value.\n\nInterpreting these lists as Mathlib polynomials over  preserves\nevaluation. Importing a Mathlib multivariate polynomial and exporting it\nagain returns the original polynomial. In the other direction, different\nlists can represent the same\npolynomial, so a raw list need not equal its own round trip.\n\nKey operations include:\n\n* Euclidean division: exact quotient and remainder satisfying\n for univariate polynomials.* Square-free factorization (squareFreeFactors?): a Yun-style candidate\ngenerator proposes factors indexed by intended multiplicity. Its output is a\nproposal; the semantic contract belongs to the radical certificate built\nfrom it.* Rational factors (RationalFactorization.split): linear and quadratic\nfactors are kept; x is split off by exact division while it divides a\nfactor of degree three or more; any other factor of degree three or more is\nsplit only if a supplied provider proposes proper factors whose product is\nthe original factor. Otherwise it stays whole. Rational integration uses\nthe list as a proposal and checks its final primitive by differentiation.* Exact evaluation (evalRat): evaluates at rational points with exact\narithmetic, used for sign determination in Sturm chains.* Interval evaluation (evalInterval): rigorous enclosure of the\npolynomial's range on a rational panel.\n\nAn exact quotient-and-remainder candidate is accepted only after sparse\npolynomial normalization proves the corresponding identity. The checked\nidentity is then projected to Mathlib's Polynomial ℚ.Every accepted certificate proves the polynomial identityin Polynomial ℚ, including the exact quotient and remainder supplied by the\ncandidate.\n\nTranslate the candidate quotient and remainder into the sparse normal form and\nnormalize . Acceptance means this difference has zero normal form.\nNormalization soundness gives the polynomial identity over the rationals,\nwhich is then transported to Mathlib's polynomial representation.\n\nA radical certificate proves that a proposed square-free reduction has exactly\nthe same real zero set as the original polynomial and has nonzero derivative\nat every root. The executable Yun-style generator returns only a certificate that\npasses the independent check of two divisibility identities and a Bézout identity.Writing the checked radical as ,\n\nThe checked division identities certify the gcd decomposition used by the\nsquare-free reduction. They show that removing repeated factors preserves\nexactly the zero set. Coprimality of the reduced polynomial and its derivative\nthen rules out a point where both vanish, so every remaining root is simple.\n\nAn accepted Sturm certificate begins with the requested polynomial and its\nformal derivative, satisfies every signed Euclidean recurrence exactly, and\nends in a polynomial that is nonzero on the real line. These are structural\nfacts about the checked chain, before any root-counting theorem is applied.For a chain , validation establishes\n\nThe independent checker verifies the first two entries and every signed\nquotient-remainder identity using exact polynomial normalization. At the\nterminal entry it checks a zero formal derivative and a nonzero value at zero.\nThe mean value theorem makes that polynomial constant, hence nowhere zero.\nCollecting these checked equalities yields the defining Sturm-chain\nconditions.\n\nFor an accepted chain and ordered rational endpoints that are not roots, the\ndrop in endpoint sign variations equals the number of distinct real roots in\nthe intervening open interval. This is the analytic Sturm theorem that gives\nthe executable variation count its mathematical meaning.\n\nAlong an interval containing no endpoint root, sign variation is locally\nconstant except when crossing a root of . The signed Euclidean recurrence\nshows that noninitial chain entries cannot create a net jump, while each\ndistinct root of  decreases the variation by one. Summing the jumps from\n to  gives the root count.\n\nA signed Euclidean chain is proposed outside the checker and accepted only\nwhen the independent structural checker SturmCertificate.valid accepts\nevery initial, recurrence, degree, and terminal condition. SymLean has no\nchain generator: a caller supplies the chain.\n\nSturm counts certify unique-root intervals and exact root counts for a checked\nsubdivision tree. An accepted finite subdivision therefore projects to the\nsame root-atlas interface used by interval and monotonicity certificates.For each accepted node  with interval ,and every leaf with count one projects to an\n certificate.\n\nApply the Sturm count theorem at every node. A node of count zero is root-free;\na node of count one contains a root and cannot contain two distinct roots.\nFor a split node, additivity of root counts over its adjacent children\npreserves the parent's exact count. Structural induction over the accepted\nsubdivision tree therefore produces the root atlas.\n\nThe isolation certificates combine square-free reduction, a Sturm\nsubdivision, transport back to the original zero set, and a checked rational\nCauchy outer bound. GlobalPolynomialIsolationCertificate.valid checks every\nstage, and the noncomputable toGlobalRootAtlas projection then yields a\nglobal atlas covering every distinct real root; a nonzero constant has an\nempty atlas. SymLean checks these certificates when a caller supplies them and\nhas no generator for them.The final global contract iswith each root-bearing region carrying a unique-root theorem.\n\nScope of the exact core.\n\nThe decision procedures of the exact core work inside its reflected real\nlanguage, with rational polynomial coefficients. A bounded search that returns\nnone has found no supported certificate within its budget; the statement\nitself may still be true.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Exact Symbolic Calculus","header":"10.6.1. Verified Exact Symbolic Computation","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Exact-Symbolic-Calculus/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Exact-Symbolic-Calculus--Verified-Exact-Symbolic-Computation"}});